<Layout title="About RateTerrain" desc="RateTerrain maps what things cost from official public data — with sources, dates, and methodology on every page." path="/about/">
<main class="wrap prose">
  <div class="hero" style="padding-bottom:8px">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>Numbers you can trace</h1>
  </div>
  <p>Search for "how much does health insurance cost" and you'll mostly find two things: articles that answer with one vague national average, and quote forms that want your phone number before showing anything. RateTerrain is the third thing — a reference site that reads the official public data and shows you the local answer, with the source and date attached.</p>
  <h2>Three rules we build by</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Public sources only.</b> Every dataset we use is published by a government agency and named on the page.</li>
    <li><b>Ranges, not false precision.</b> Costs are landscapes, not points — we show you the terrain: lowest, typical, and how your area compares.</li>
    <li><b>Dated and versioned.</b> Data ages. Each page says when its numbers were imported, and our <a href="/texas/health-insurance-cost/methodology/">methodology</a> explains exactly how they're computed.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Where we are on the map</h2>
  <p>We started where the data is strongest and the confusion greatest: <a href="/texas/health-insurance-cost/">Texas health insurance</a>. More cost terrains — more states, more kinds of everyday costs — are being surveyed.</p>
  <h2>What we are not</h2>
  <p>Not an insurance agency, not a comparison site paid to rank one company above another, and not affiliated with any government body. We're a map. The decisions stay yours.</p>
</main>
</Layout>
